# Prisma and database standards

## Schema conventions

- Model names in PascalCase, singular: `User`, `Document`, `DocumentField`.
- Field names in camelCase: `createdAt`, `clinicId`, `originalPdfKey`.
- Primary keys are `id String @id @default(uuid())`. Do not use auto-increment integers.
- Every model has `createdAt DateTime @default(now())`. Mutable models add `updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt`.
- Foreign keys named `<entity>Id`: `clinicId`, `documentId`.
- Relation fields named after the referenced entity: `clinic Clinic @relation(...)`.
- Enum types in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE values, PascalCase names: `DocumentStatus`, `FieldType`.

## Indexes

- Every foreign key that is queried gets an index: `@@index([clinicId])`.
- Compound indexes for common filter combinations: `@@index([clinicId, status])`.
- Unique constraints as `@unique` on single field or `@@unique([a, b])` for composites.
- Do not over-index. Every index costs writes.

## Migrations

- Use `prisma migrate dev --name <descriptive_name>` in development.
- Migration names describe the change: `add_audit_log_table`, `add_document_expires_at`, not `update1`.
- Never hand-edit migration SQL after it has been applied in any environment.
- Use `prisma migrate deploy` in production. Never `migrate dev` in prod.
- Commit migration files to git.

## Prisma Client setup

- Single PrismaClient instance for the app lifetime, exported from `src/lib/prisma.ts`.
- In dev, preserve the client across hot reloads to avoid connection leaks:

```ts
import { PrismaClient } from "@prisma/client";

const globalForPrisma = globalThis as unknown as { prisma?: PrismaClient };

export const prisma =
  globalForPrisma.prisma ??
  new PrismaClient({
    log: ["error", "warn"],
  });

if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== "production") {
  globalForPrisma.prisma = prisma;
}
```

- Configure log levels per environment. Log queries in dev only.

## Queries

- Select only the fields you need with `select: { ... }`. Do not load entire rows when you need three columns.
- Use `include` for relations only when the caller needs them. Otherwise paginate separately.
- Paginate lists with `take` and `skip` or cursor-based pagination. Never load unbounded lists.
- Use `findUnique` for single lookups on unique fields. `findFirst` only when you genuinely need ordering or multi-field matching.
- Avoid N+1 by using `include` or splitting queries intentionally.

## Transactions

- Use `prisma.$transaction` when multiple writes must succeed together or all roll back.
- Interactive transactions (with a callback) for logic that depends on reads within the transaction:

```ts
const result = await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  const doc = await tx.document.create({ data: ... });
  await tx.auditLog.create({ data: { documentId: doc.id, ... } });
  return doc;
});
```

- Array transactions for simple batched writes: `prisma.$transaction([op1, op2])`.
- Set isolation level explicitly for contested operations: `{ isolationLevel: "Serializable" }`.
- Keep transactions short. No network calls, no S3, no email inside a transaction.

## Soft deletes

- Prefer status transitions over hard deletes: `status = "VOIDED"` instead of `DELETE FROM`.
- If you must hard delete, do it in a repository method named `hardDelete` to make the intent explicit.
- Cascading deletes configured at the schema level with `onDelete: Cascade` when the child makes no sense without the parent.

## Audit logging

- Every state change writes an `AuditLog` row. This is append-only.
- Never UPDATE or DELETE an audit log entry. There is no repository method for it.
- Audit log includes: `documentId`, `actorType`, `actorId`, `eventType`, `metadata` (JSON), `timestamp`.
- Metadata JSON captures context: IP, user agent, field values that were changed, previous status. Never include raw passwords or tokens.

## Connection pooling

- Configure `DATABASE_URL` with connection pool params: `?connection_limit=10&pool_timeout=20`.
- For serverless or spiky workloads, use PgBouncer or Prisma Accelerate.
- In RDS, set `max_connections` appropriately for the instance size.

## Seeding

- Seed script at `apps/api/prisma/seed.ts` creates deterministic demo data.
- Include: 1 clinic, 1 provider user (email `demo@clinicsign.test`, password `DemoPass123`), 1 draft document.
- Seed is idempotent: running twice should not create duplicates. Use `upsert` or check existence first.
- Never seed real PHI or real email addresses.

## Repository pattern

- One repository per aggregate root: `user.repository.ts`, `document.repository.ts`, `audit-log.repository.ts`.
- Repositories expose domain-oriented methods, not generic CRUD. Prefer `findPendingForClinic(clinicId)` over `findMany(where)`.
- Pass an optional transaction client as the last parameter:

```ts
async function createDocument(
  data: CreateDocumentInput,
  tx?: Prisma.TransactionClient
): Promise<Document> {
  const client = tx ?? prisma;
  return client.document.create({ data });
}
```

## Data types

- Money and decimals: `Decimal` in Prisma. Never store money as `Float`.
- Timestamps: always `DateTime`. Store in UTC.
- Text that could be large (PDF content, signatures): store in S3, keep only the key in DB.
- JSON fields: typed with Prisma `Json` type and a Zod schema on both read and write.

## Things never do with Prisma

- Never call Prisma from a controller or service without going through a repository
- Never store PDFs, images, or large blobs in the database
- Never hard delete data that might be audited later
- Never use raw SQL unless there is a documented reason (put it in a repository with a comment explaining why)
- Never share Prisma client instances across requests via globals (except in dev as shown above)
- Never run `migrate dev --create-only` without reviewing the generated SQL
- Never commit an unreviewed generated migration
